course: biology
lesson: 205
title: Budgies and canaries
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Budgies, short for budgerigars, are small parrots from the dry grasslands of Australia, where they fly in huge flocks that look like a green cloud. A pet budgie is a flock bird living in a house.
explain: That is why a budgie chatters, whistles and bobs its head all day, why it stares into a mirror as if at a friend, and why it is happiest with another budgie beside it.
explain: Canaries are small finches whose males are famous for their songs; long ago coal miners carried them underground because a canary showed the effects of bad gas before people noticed it.
explain: Small birds have very delicate lungs, need to fly every day and need more than seeds, so a good owner watches them closely: a fluffed-up, quiet bird may be ill.
fact: Budgies are very chatty birds that chatter and whistle a lot, and some pet budgies have learnt to say hundreds of words.
fact: All wild budgies are green and yellow; the blue, white and other colours were bred in pet budgies.
fact: You can usually tell an adult male budgie from a female by the cere, the bare patch above the beak: often blue in males and brown or pale in females; young budgies have stripes on their foreheads that fade as they grow.
fact: A budgie staring at itself in a mirror may treat the reflection like another bird, and budgies are happier with a real budgie as a friend.
fact: Head-bobbing usually means a budgie is excited or showing off, and it can also be a sign that it wants food.
fact: Male canaries sing the most, and coal miners once took canaries underground because the birds were harmed by dangerous gas before the miners noticed it.
fact: Birds' lungs are very sensitive, so cooking fumes and smoke can harm them, and fumes from overheated non-stick pans can kill small birds; cages should not be kept in the kitchen.
fact: Pet birds need time out of the cage to fly every day in a safe room, with windows and mirrors covered or marked so the bird does not fly into the glass, and other pets kept out.
fact: An all-seed diet is not enough for a budgie; fresh vegetables and pellets help keep it healthy.
fact: A budgie that sits fluffed up and still for a long time may be ill and needs a vet quickly, because small birds hide illness until they are very sick.
fact: A budgie grinding its beak softly at bedtime is usually content and relaxed, and budgies often sleep standing on one leg with the head tucked into the back feathers.
exercise: A budgie chatters and whistles in its cage. Is this normal? | Yes, budgies are very chatty birds | No, it means it is ill and must see a vet | No, budgies are silent birds | Yes, but only at night
exercise: How many words can some pet budgies learn? | Hundreds | None at all | Exactly one | Over a million
exercise: What colour is a wild budgie flock? | Green and yellow | Bright blue | Pure white | Red and black
exercise: Where do wild budgies come from? | The grasslands of Australia | The jungles of Brazil | The ice of the Arctic | The mountains of Scotland
exercise: How can you tell an adult boy budgie from a girl budgie? | By the colour of the cere above the beak | By the length of its tail feathers | By how loud it sings | By counting its toes, because boys have more
exercise: What are the stripes on a young budgie's forehead? | Baby markings that fade as it grows | A sign of illness | Paint from the pet shop | Scars from fighting with the older birds in the flock
exercise: A budgie stares at itself in a mirror. What might it think? | That it is another bird | That it is a cat | That it is a window to outside | Nothing, budgies cannot see mirrors
exercise: What makes a budgie happiest? | Another budgie as a friend | A mirror instead of a friend | A dark room | A cage of its own forever
exercise: A budgie bobs its head up and down. What can it mean? | It is excited, showing off or wants food | It has a headache | It is about to fall asleep on its perch | It is angry at the cage and wants to break out of it
exercise: A canary sings in a cage. Which canaries sing most? | The males | The females | The babies | The old ones only
exercise: Why did coal miners once take canaries underground? | The birds showed the effects of dangerous gas before people noticed it | To sing the miners to sleep | To find gold | To eat the insects in the mine and keep the tunnels clean for the men working there
exercise: A bird cage sits near the kitchen. Why is that a problem? | Cooking fumes and smoke can harm birds' lungs | Birds hate the smell of food and stop singing | The cage gets too dirty | Birds will eat all the food left on the table
exercise: Which fumes can kill small birds? | Fumes from overheated non-stick pans | The smell of fresh bread | Steam from a kettle in another room | Air from an open window
exercise: A budgie flies around the room. What must be done first? | Cover windows and mirrors and keep other pets out | Open all the windows so it has plenty of fresh air | Turn on the ceiling fan to cool it down | Put the cat in the room
exercise: A budgie gets seeds only. Is this enough? | No, it also needs vegetables and pellets | Yes, seeds are all it needs to stay healthy | Yes, if they are big seeds | No, it needs meat as well
exercise: A budgie fluffs up its feathers and sits still for a long time. What should you do? | Take it to a vet quickly, it may be ill | Nothing, it is happy and just having a rest | Give it a bath | Play loud music to wake it
exercise: A budgie grinds its beak at night. What does it mean? | It is content and relaxed | It has toothache | It is very angry | It is hungry
exercise: How do budgies often sleep? | Standing on one leg with the head tucked into the back feathers | Lying on their backs | Hanging upside down from the top of the cage like bats in a cave | In a hole in the sand
test: «my budgie's blue so wild budgies must be blue too» What is right? | Wild budgies are all green and yellow; blue was bred in pets | Yes, all wild budgies are blue, just like the sky they fly about in | Wild budgies are every colour | Wild budgies are black
test: «he chats to the mirror all day, so he's got a mate, sorted» What is right? | A real budgie friend is much better than a mirror | A mirror is all he needs | Budgies hate other budgies | Mirrors make budgies sing like canaries, and that is the same as having a friend
test: «Pip's all puffed up and quiet since yesterday, prob just cold lol» What should happen? | Take him to a vet soon, a fluffed-up quiet bird may be ill | Leave him another week | Give him a cold bath | Put him in the kitchen near the hot oven to warm him up quickly
test: «keep the cage in the kitchen so he's got company while we cook» What is wrong with this? | Cooking fumes and smoke can harm or even kill small birds | Birds get too fat in kitchens from all the crumbs they are given | Birds hate company | Nothing at all is wrong
test: «budgie's grinding his beak at bedtime, is he angry??» What is right? | It usually means he is content and relaxed | He is furious with you for turning off the light | He has toothache | He is sharpening it for a fight
test: «mum says seeds are bird food so seeds only, end of» What is right? | Budgies need vegetables and pellets too, not only seeds | Seeds only is perfect | Budgies should eat crisps too | Budgies need meat every day, like the wild birds in the garden
test: In a cartoon a canary teases a cat and sings in every scene. What is true of real canaries? | Male canaries sing most, and they must be kept safe from cats | Female canaries sing most | Canaries are brave enough to scare cats away by singing at them | Canaries never sing in real life
test: What would happen if a budgie flew around a room with an uncovered big window? | It might fly into the glass and get hurt | It would stop at the glass perfectly | It would fly through the glass | It would never fly near it
test: Count them: 3 budgies and 2 canaries in an aviary, a big bird cage. How many wings? | 10 | 5 | 20 | 8
test: Count them: two budgies sleep, each standing on one leg. How many feet are on the perch, and how many tucked up? | 2 on the perch and 2 tucked up | 4 on the perch and none tucked up | 1 on the perch and 3 tucked up | 2 on the perch and 4 tucked up
test: «how do I know if mine's a boy or a girl» Which clue helps with an adult budgie? | The colour of the cere above the beak | The number of feathers in its tail | Whether it likes seeds | The colour of its feet only
test: «why did old miners take a little yellow bird down the pit» What is the answer? | It was harmed by bad gas before people noticed it, so it warned them | It sang to keep them awake | It found coal for them by tapping on the rock walls with its little beak | It ate the rats
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the colouration of wild budgerigars? | Wild budgerigars are uniformly green and yellow, other colours having arisen through breeding | Wild budgerigars are predominantly blue, the green birds having arisen solely through breeding in captivity | Wild budgerigars occur in every colour | Wild budgerigars are entirely white
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a bird cage ought not to be situated in a kitchen. | Fumes and smoke from cooking may injure or kill small birds | Birds are invariably averse to human company | The odour of food induces excessive singing | Kitchens are insufficiently illuminated
exam: It is given that a budgerigar has remained fluffed up and motionless for a prolonged period. Ascertain the appropriate course of action. | Veterinary advice ought to be sought without delay | The bird ought to be bathed in cold water | No action is required, as this denotes contentment | The bird ought to be confined in darkness for a week
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the diet of the budgerigar. | Seeds alone are insufficient, and vegetables and pellets are also required | A diet consisting solely of seeds is ideal for the health of the bird | Meat ought to be supplied daily | Budgerigars require no food whatsoever when kept indoors in winter
